{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of trained non-experts with a checklist is more reliable in expectation, but genuine reliability is proven only when the panel exhibits lower error variance than the expert without elevated systematic error on configural, checklist-silent items.","counterconditions":["If the panel's residual error is systematically aligned with the checklist's known cue-omissions, the gain is compliance-tracking, not genuine reliability.","If the expert's variance is low (high consistency across bootstrapped subsets of cases), the panel's variance-reduction advantage vanishes and panel is not more reliable.","If the task is dominated by configural integration not decomposable into checklist items, the checklist bias exceeds expert variance and the panel is worse."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklist+panel and intuition+expert map onto a bias-variance tradeoff: aggregation reduces variance, but the checklist injects bias by compressing the cue-space. Panel>expert iff the variance reduction exceeds the checklist's bias. In a single evaluation, decomposing the total error reveals whether the panel's advantage is variance-driven (genuine) or merely bias-tracking (compliance that happens to match the oracle).","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","648235daac74446e25fd323079b30acc8c38c9f516f8de8713ad82b5a9221897","6c697a16f23a03891ed84f42f24d0d9b37b02f7ba1c9467f6e73718bc0097904"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel is NOT more reliable, because checklist compliance is a lossy compression of expert judgment that cannot survive tasks requiring configural integration of cues. Genuine reliability would require the panel to match the expert on interaction-heavy items, which a flat checklist structurally cannot deliver.","counterconditions":["If the checklist itself encodes configural rules (interaction terms), the 'flat checklist' assumption fails and the panel may be reliable.","If the task is purely additive (no configural integration needed), the checklist is not lossy and the panel may genuinely outperform.","If the expert also fails on configural items (intuition is not configural either), the disanalogy between checklist and expert vanishes."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A checklist encodes additive, independent cues. Configural tasks require judgment of cue-interactions (e.g., cue A is predictive only when cue B is absent). The panel, following the checklist, cannot represent this and will fail systematically. In a single evaluation, the distinguishing evidence is structured, correlated error on interaction items: if the panel errs specifically and predictably when cues interact, its overall accuracy is mere compliance, not reliability.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","53e9504c5191b6075aa950e34eb772d1c433af167a0e6ce97fa4b5d2ab61c505"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel is more reliable, but ONLY IF the checklist was not authored by a single expert but was crowd-evolved (e.g., Delphi with outcome feedback), reducing checklist bias below the variance of any single expert. Distinguishing genuine gain from compliance requires probing items where the checklist and its author disagree with the broader expert population.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is single-author, the bias-equals-expert-bias problem returns and the panel offers only variance reduction.","If crowd-evolution converges on a shallow consensus cue-set, the checklist may still miss configural cues.","If the evaluation lacks items that discriminate between single-author and crowd-evolved checklists, the evidence is uninformative."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the checklist is a lossy compression of one expert, its bias equals that expert's bias, and aggregation adds no new information. But if the checklist is crowd-evolved, its cue-set can exceed any single expert's, lowering bias. In a single evaluation, the distinguishing evidence is performance on items where the checklist diverges from a single-author proxy: if the panel succeeds where a single-author checklist would fail, the gain is genuine bias-reduction, not compliance-tracking.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","648235daac74446e25fd323079b30acc8c38c9f516f8de8713ad82b5a9221897"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Neither is reliably superior: the panel and the expert succeed and fail on orthogonal task-classes. The panel wins on high-variance, low-configural tasks; the expert wins on low-variance, high-configural tasks. Genuine reliability gain is identified by matching the error profile to the task structure within a single evaluation.","counterconditions":["If configural demand is not prespecified, the partitioning is post-hoc and unfalsifiable.","If the expert also succeeds on high-variance low-configural tasks, the panel has no domain of advantage.","If the task distribution is unknown, expected reliability cannot be computed from a single evaluation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Panel+checklist is a low-variance, high-bias-on-configural estimator. Expert+intuition is a high-variance, low-bias-on-configural estimator. Reliability is conditional on task demands. In a single evaluation, partition cases by configural demand (pre-specified). If the panel's advantage is concentrated on low-configural items and vanishes on high-configural items, the 'gain' is task-specific variance reduction, not a general reliability improvement.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","261f9c7ce501c27e38e7e2afca73d8e90637cf9597f3987e9867e2cddf764d56","53e9504c5191b6075aa950e34eb772d1c433af167a0e6ce97fa4b5d2ab61c505"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel is more reliable because checklist compliance IS a reliability gain: replacing unobserved intuition with an auditable procedure eliminates the expert's opaque variance. The compliance-vs-genuine distinction is a false dichotomy; compliant tracking of the right answer is what reliability means for verifiable tasks.","counterconditions":["If the panel's compliance tracks the right answer in-sample but fails on held-out items drawn from the same verifiable distribution, the gain was overfitting, not reliability.","If the task distribution shifts, compliance without configural understanding fails, revealing the distinction was real.","If the evaluation asks about future reliability (not just current accuracy), process-evaluation is necessary and the dichotomy returns."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"For tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, 'genuine reliability' and 'compliance that tracks the right answer' collapse into the same thing: low error. The distinction only matters for un-verifiable tasks where process-evaluation is needed. For verifiable tasks, if the panel's compliance yields lower error, that is by definition a reliability gain. The search for 'genuine' vs 'compliance' evidence is looking for a ghost.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","6c697a16f23a03891ed84f42f24d0d9b37b02f7ba1c9467f6e73718bc0097904"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Within a single evaluation, the bias-variance frame is not directly instantiable because separating bias from variance requires oracle access to the true generative model. Instead, genuine reliability gain is identified by bootstrap-internal consistency: if the panel's error is stable under resampling of items while the expert's error fluctuates, the panel has genuine (variance-driven) reliability without needing to decompose bias.","counterconditions":["If the evaluation has too few items for stable bootstrap estimates, the variance comparison is unreliable.","If the expert is also highly consistent (low variance), the bootstrap test is null and cannot distinguish the estimators.","If the panel's low variance is paired with high bias (e.g., always wrong in the same way), lower variance alone does not prove reliability."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The bias-variance decomposition (SCR_002) is elegant but requires knowing the true function to separate bias from variance empirically. A single evaluation lacks this. However, error variance CAN be estimated via bootstrap resampling of cases: if the panel's accuracy is stable under resampling but the expert's is not, the panel has lower variance. If the panel's MEAN error is also not elevated relative to the expert, the gain is genuine. This sidesteps the oracle problem.","optional_refs":["648235daac74446e25fd323079b30acc8c38c9f516f8de8713ad82b5a9221897","261f9c7ce501c27e38e7e2afca73d8e90637cf9597f3987e9867e2cddf764d56","6c697a16f23a03891ed84f42f24d0d9b37b02f7ba1c9467f6e73718bc0097904"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"Panelists can recognize when a checklist is insufficient even if they cannot solve the configural item themselves.","relation_hint":"NEW_001 addresses SCR_004 by proposing that configural tasks can be detected within a single evaluation via panelist self-report of checklist-insufficiency, rather than requiring a separate OOD probe.","to_ref":"SCR_004","weakens_when":"Non-expert panelists lack the metacognitive skill to recognize checklist-insufficiency."}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_007"],"content":"Counterfactual: If the panel is given the checklist AND the expert's individual judgments (not just aggregation), the expert could serve as a configural probe. But this changes the comparison. Alternatively: what if the panel is allowed to DEVIATE from the checklist with justification? Then genuine reliability would be shown by deviations that improve accuracy on configural items. This suggests an evaluation design where panelists can flag 'checklist-insufficient' and provide free-text judgment, scored against the oracle.","possible_next_move":"Design a protocol where panelists rate checklist-sufficiency per item and the correlation between insufficiency-ratings and configural-item errors is measured.","why_keep_this":"It offers a concrete single-evaluation design that tests for configural judgment without requiring OOD probes: let the panel self-report checklist-insufficiency and evaluate whether deviations are predictive of correctness."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can bootstrap resampling of items within a single evaluation reliably separate error variance from error bias, or does it require the panel and expert to face enough items that the Central Limit Theorem applies to the error distribution?","related_refs":["SCR_003","SCR_002"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}